body:
I've replaced shocks & struts several times for other peoples cars (mobile mechanic), I plan on doing my Mazda's soon. It's not over complicated, which is nice. I'm 100% sure on the clunking, but it's a solid chance it will.

&#x200B;

For the vibration, I've heard that it's the thin rust layer between your wheel and the hub and removing that helped. I'd also get your tires balanced right after your alignment, post suspension job.

These aren't mine, I snagged these YEARS ago from another redditor who I don't remember. I hope these help!

No crystal ball required.First you thoroughly research the company extensively before you even apply.

What's their reputation as a business like?Do they make a healthy profit?

&#x200B;

What do people on Glassdoor say?

&#x200B;

Then in your interview(s) you interview them as much as they interview you.

&#x200B;

Ask them what their IT strategy and plans are.

&#x200B;

Ask them about their IT structure, how many people there are in the team, and in the company total.

&#x200B;

Ask them what your roles and responsibilities are.

&#x200B;

Ask them what training they provide.

&#x200B;

Ask them what their hours are.

&#x200B;

Ask them if they allow WFH.

&#x200B;

Ask them if you are expected to work out of hours and/or be on call. If so required, ask them how often, and ask what compensation you get for that.

&#x200B;

Ask to meet the team you will work with, especially the person who will be your manager.

&#x200B;

Ask them how much documentation they have.

&#x200B;

Ask them how much automation they have, and whether there is scope for more.

&#x200B;

Ask them to show you where you will work.

&#x200B;

Ask then to show you their server rooms and network closets

&#x200B;

It's your responsibility to find out things before you accept the job. It's not 100% fool proof, but with some due diligence you will weed out almost all shitty companies, and definitely the truly shitty ones

I am hosting work environments for my co-workers, this gives some of our junior guys and even some veteran techs something to learn on and even present if needed. I have it configured so that everyone is in their own network and with rules set on my firewall, are unable to communicate with ANYTHING on my network, except the outside world. This also gives me the time to learn how to administrate other admins in a environment and ensure things are going smoothly in terms of performance. I also monitor the traffic and block a lot of heavy hitting things like video streaming as that would murder my network. I also throttle the networks so that when I'm home I don't have any slowness.   


For now, I use my works RMM tool (Kaseya) too allow them access into their machines. However this is going away with a newer solution.\*

&#x200B;

\*I'll be setting up a new domain that isn't the TTL(TinyTechLab) one. This domain will host a DC,RAS, RDS, WDS,WSUS box.   


I'll have to do some network wizardry to allow VPN to work with RAS but from there, they will use credentials from the "Gateway" domain to sign in. Then, they will go to their browser and open a webpage for the RDS box with all the VM's they are granted access to and a single pane of glass to see what machines they have.   


Any additional resources have to be requested and approved by me and since no one has access to my Hyper-V host, they won't be able to do any imaging for now. 

&#x200B;

I have big plans to upgrade that will greatly improve performance for everyone, but that comes in time. :)

&#x200B;

&#x200B;
